      "content": "Yes, that's correct - the repulsion between lecithin headgroups can lead to increased droplet growth and coalescence. To counteract this, you may consider using emulsifiers with different headgroups, such as polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) or poloxamer 108, which can form stronger electrostatic interactions or van der Waals bridges with lecithin, stabilizing the interface.\n\nAlternatively, you can also modify the lecithin concentration by optimizing its ratio to other ingredients, or by adjusting the concentration of added counter-ions, such as calcium or magnesium ions, which can enhance the electrostatic stability of the lecithin layer and reduce coalescence.",
